WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.560
A repeatable workflow begins with authorization, mission planning, aircraft assignment, weather and airspace review, crew briefing, equipment preparation, and preflight inspection.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:16.146
Each step should have a clear owner and a record appropriate to the risk.

00:00:16.146 --> 00:00:24.857
At launch, establish a controlled area, confirm communications, verify aircraft status, and climb only after stable control is clear.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:33.992
The team should know the initial route, first decision point, and the words used to call an abort or landing.

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:41.003
During flight, the pilot monitors aircraft position, link quality, battery, weather, hazards, and surrounding traffic.

00:00:41.003 --> 00:00:46.739
The payload operator or observer supports mission tasks without distracting from safety.

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:50.775
Record deviations and significant events as they occur.

00:00:50.775 --> 00:00:56.511
Recovery is a planned phase, not the moment the battery becomes urgent.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:03.522
Clear the landing area, announce the approach, maintain margins, and secure the aircraft after touchdown.

00:01:03.522 --> 00:01:08.408
Treat damaged or abnormal batteries according to approved safety procedures.

00:01:08.408 --> 00:01:19.243
Post-flight work includes inspecting the aircraft, recording maintenance concerns, accounting for batteries and media, offloading data into approved storage, and completing the flight log.

00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:24.979
Do not erase or reformat original media until handling requirements are satisfied.

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:31.565
An after-action review asks what happened, what worked, what changed, and what should improve.

00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:35.602
Update checklists, training, risk controls, and maintenance actions.

00:01:35.602 --> 00:01:41.338
A disciplined workflow turns each flight into evidence for safer future operations.
